Here are the exact findings.

## 1. DB schema SQL files defining the `owners` table

All four schema variants declare both `namesake_count` and `household_id`. The `data.sql` files do NOT create the table (only inserts), so only these four matter for adding a column:

**`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/resources/db/hsqldb/schema.sql`** (lines 38-52, note: no `IF NOT EXISTS`):
```sql
CREATE TABLE owners (
  id         INTEGER IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,
  first_name VARCHAR(30),
  last_name  VARCHAR_IGNORECASE(30),
  address    VARCHAR(255),
  city       VARCHAR(80),
  telephone  VARCHAR(20),
  email      VARCHAR(255),
  registration_date DATE,
  customer_code VARCHAR(20),
  household_id VARCHAR(20),
  namesake_count INTEGER,
  bulk_signup_warning BOOLEAN
);
CREATE INDEX owners_last_name ON owners (last_name);
```

**`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/resources/db/h2/schema.sql`** (lines 31-46):
```sql
CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS owners (
  id INTEGER GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,
  first_name VARCHAR(30) NOT NULL,
  last_name VARCHAR(30) NOT NULL,
  address VARCHAR(255) NOT NULL,
  city VARCHAR(80) NOT NULL,
  telephone VARCHAR(20) NOT NULL,
  email VARCHAR(255),
  registration_date DATE,
  customer_code VARCHAR(20),
  household_id VARCHAR(20),
  namesake_count INTEGER,
  bulk_signup_warning BOOLEAN
);
CREATE INDEX idx_owners_last_name ON owners(last_name);
```

**`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/resources/db/mysql/schema.sql`** (lines 28-42):
```sql
CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS owners (
  id INT(4) UNSIGNED NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT PRIMARY KEY,
  first_name VARCHAR(30),
  last_name VARCHAR(30),
  address VARCHAR(255),
  city VARCHAR(80),
  telephone VARCHAR(20),
  email VARCHAR(255),
  registration_date DATE,
  customer_code VARCHAR(20),
  household_id VARCHAR(20),
  namesake_count INTEGER,
  bulk_signup_warning BOOLEAN,
  INDEX(last_name)
) engine=InnoDB;
```

**`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/resources/db/postgres/schema.sql`** (lines 26-40):
```sql
CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS owners (
    id         INT GENERATED BY DEFAULT AS IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,
    first_name TEXT,
    last_name  TEXT,
    address    TEXT,
    city       TEXT,
    telephone  TEXT,
    email      TEXT,
    registration_date DATE,
    customer_code TEXT,
    household_id TEXT,
    namesake_count INTEGER,
    bulk_signup_warning BOOLEAN
);
CREATE INDEX ON owners (last_name);
```

There are no Liquibase/Flyway migration files in this repo (no `db/changelog`, no `db/migration`, no versioned migrations) — schema is managed solely by these four `schema.sql` files. So a new column must be added to all four.

## 2. OwnerDto / MembershipTierEnum

`OwnerDto` is **not checked into source** — it is generated at build time by the OpenAPI generator. Config in **`/home/daniel/sandbox/pom.xml`** (~lines 350-368):
- `modelNameSuffix` = `Dto`
- `modelPackage` = `org.springframework.samples.petclinic.rest.dto`
- generator plugin version 7.23.0

So the class resolves to `org.springframework.samples.petclinic.rest.dto.OwnerDto` with a nested `OwnerDto.MembershipTierEnum`.

The spec that drives generation is **`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/resources/openapi.yml`** (membershipTier at lines 2127-2135):
```yaml
            membershipTier:
              title: Membership tier
              description: "'SILVER' when the owner has no namesakes (namesakeCount is 0) and an email is present, otherwise 'BRONZE'."
              type: string
              enum:
                - BRONZE
                - SILVER
              example: 'SILVER'
              readOnly: true
```

Important: the enum in the spec has only **BRONZE** and **SILVER** — there is **no GOLD** value. The generated `MembershipTierEnum` therefore has only `BRONZE` and `SILVER`. The mapper confirms this in **`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/mapper/OwnerMapper.java`** (lines 74-78):
```java
default OwnerDto.MembershipTierEnum resolveMembershipTier(Owner owner) {
    boolean noNamesakes = Integer.valueOf(0).equals(owner.getNamesakeCount());
    boolean hasEmail = owner.getEmail() != null && !owner.getEmail().isBlank();
    return noNamesakes && hasEmail ? OwnerDto.MembershipTierEnum.SILVER : OwnerDto.MembershipTierEnum.BRONZE;
}
```

GOLD is only referenced (and expected) by a test that is not yet supported by the spec/enum — **`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/test/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/acceptance/AcceptanceTest.java`** (lines 7-9, 28): asserts `$.membershipTier` == `"GOLD"` for a 3+ member household. To make GOLD real you'd need to add it to the openapi.yml enum (and the mapper logic).

## 3. `existsOwnerInHousehold`

Interface declaration — **`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/service/ClinicService.java`** line 62:
```java
boolean existsOwnerInHousehold(String lastName, String address) throws DataAccessException;
```

Implementation — **`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/service/ClinicServiceImpl.java`** lines 258-262:
```java
@Override
@Transactional(readOnly = true)
public boolean existsOwnerInHousehold(String lastName, String address) throws DataAccessException {
    return !findHouseholdMembers(lastName, address).isEmpty();
}
```
(`findHouseholdMembers` is a private helper starting at line 339 of the same file.)

## 4. Full owner-related `ClinicService` interface declarations

**`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/service/ClinicService.java`** lines 57-70:
```java
